@node CMacs Gnuseye
@chapter CMacs Gnuseye: GNU's Eye Live Planetary Globe

@cindex GNU's Eye
@cindex gnuseye
@cindex globe

GNU's Eye (@code{gnuseye}) is a Google-Earth / Palantir-style live 3D globe
embedded in a cmacs buffer.  It overlays trackable objects --- satellites
and spacecraft, aircraft, marine vessels, weather and geo-events, natural
events, weather alert zones, aurora, geolocated news --- as clickable,
heading-oriented markers, filled regions, rings, and arcs on a sun-lit,
politically-mapped sphere.  It is rendered @emph{through} the libregnum
subsystem and enabled with @option{--with-cmacs-gnuseye} (requires
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} and @option{--with-cmacs-glib}).  It is part
of the standard cmacs flag set: @command{just bootstrap} and the container
build enable it by default.

@code{M-x cmacs-gnuseye} opens a dashboard: the globe viewport in the
centre; on the left an entity-type toggle pane over a searchable/filterable
entity list; and an inspector on the right.  Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom,
right-drag to pan, hover a marker to identify it, and click (or select its
list row) to recentre on it and fill the inspector.  @code{s} searches by
name; @code{F} filters by kind on both the list and the globe; @code{l}
opens the type pane.  Navigation works in vanilla Emacs and Evil/Doom (the
viewport runs in Evil @code{emacs} state, the panels in @code{motion}
state).

@section Entity-type categories

The top-left pane organises layers into @emph{collapsible categories} (Air
traffic, Astronomical, Marine, Weather & events, Natural events, Space
weather, Conflict & geopolitics, Infrastructure, Health & society, Media &
cameras, Markets).  A category header shows @code{@U{25B8}}/@code{@U{25BE}}
and a count of how many of its layers are on; @code{SPC}/@code{RET} on a
header expands or collapses it, on a layer toggles that layer.
@strong{Everything starts off and every category starts collapsed}, so the
globe opens clean (map only) and you opt into exactly the feeds you want.
Order and titles are configurable via @code{cmacs-gnuseye-group-order} and
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-group-titles}.

The globe itself is a sun-lit 3D sphere with a real political map ---
coastlines, country borders, country name labels, admin-1
states/provinces, and country flags --- from Natural Earth + flagcdn
(downloaded + cached once) and drawn in the globe's own lat/lon convention
so it always aligns with the markers.

@section Globe visualization

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Day/night terminator.}  The subsolar point is computed for the
current time and fed to the globe shader, so the lit hemisphere tracks the
real time of day (refreshed every @code{cmacs-gnuseye-sun-interval} s; toggle
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-day-night}).  The night side stays partly lit with a cool
tint so markers remain readable.
@item @strong{Filled polygons.}  Translucent regions draped on the sphere ---
weather @emph{alert zones}, aurora bands, country @emph{choropleth} fills,
AOIs.  An entity may carry a @code{:polygon} ring (per-tick, cleared with the
markers); persistent fills use @code{cmacs-gnuseye-add-polygon} with
@var{persistent} non-nil.  Fills are triangulated as a polar grid of
great-circle rings so they drape at any size, alpha-blended and two-sided.
@item @strong{Range / coverage rings.}  @code{cmacs-gnuseye-circle-points}
(built on @code{cmacs-gnuseye-destination}) returns a small-circle about a
point --- satellite footprints, detection radii, geofence circles --- usable
as an outline or a filled footprint.
@item @strong{Great-circle routes.}  @code{cmacs-gnuseye-great-circle} samples
a route (trade lanes, cables, flight paths) for a @code{:trail}.
@item @strong{Choropleth.}  @code{cmacs-gnuseye-choropleth} fills countries by
a scalar map (ISO-A3 @result{} 0..1) through a green@result{}red translucent
ramp; the intelligence layer's country-instability index drives it.
@item @strong{Low-zoom clustering.}  Dense point layers declared
@code{:cluster} (aircraft, vessels, GDELT) collapse nearby markers into a
single count badge when zoomed out; clicking a badge flies in and dissolves
it.  Toggle @code{cmacs-gnuseye-cluster}.
@end itemize

@section Layer lifecycle and motion

@strong{Atomic disable.}  Toggling a type off cancels its refresh timer,
drops its entities from the index, and clears all its markers, arcs, and
per-tick polygons immediately.  A @emph{generation token} on each layer is
bumped on every enable and disable; every async fetch captures the token at
dispatch, and a response that arrives after the layer was toggled finds a
stale token and is dropped.  A disabled layer can never be re-drawn by an
in-flight update --- no ghost markers.

@strong{Dead reckoning.}  Every moving layer declares an @code{:advance}
hook.  Between the (slower) fetches the shared smooth-motion tick
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-smooth}, every @code{cmacs-gnuseye-smooth-interval}) glides
each entity along its heading + speed; the next fetch course-corrects it.
Aircraft and vessels great-circle dead-reckon
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-dead-reckon-layer}); satellites re-propagate their orbit.

@section Extensibility

Data sources are @emph{layers defined in Elisp} via
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-define-layer}, over a small stable C boundary.  A layer
supplies an async @code{:fetch} returning a list of entity plists; the C core
renders whatever entities a layer pushes and stashes each as its marker's
pick payload.  Adding a feed is one macro call --- no rebuild.  The macro
keys are @code{:title :group :kind :fetch :interval :default-on :detail
:needs-key :advance :cluster}.

The entity plist requires only @code{:id}, @code{:lat}, and @code{:lon};
optional keys include @code{:alt}, @code{:heading}, @code{:speed},
@code{:kind}, @code{:label}, @code{:color}, @code{:scale}, @code{:trail},
@code{:polygon}, @code{:label-mode}, @code{:detail}, @code{:data}, and
@code{:ts}.  See @file{doc_org/cmacs/gnuseye/reference.org} and
@file{extending.org} for the full schema, every DEFUN, and worked recipes.

@section Built-in layers

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{satellites} --- CelesTrak TLE + local two-body propagation
(orbit trails); no key.
@item @code{aircraft} --- adsb.lol ADS-B (keyless), worldwide sweep, indexed
and searchable; clustered when zoomed out.  @code{cmacs-gnuseye-air-global},
@code{-air-source}.
@item @code{vessels} --- live AIS from @code{cmacs-gnuseye-marine-url}; the
default is the keyless Digitraffic open AIS (Baltic / Gulf of Finland
coverage).  Dead-reckoned, clustered.  Point the URL at any other regional
feed (GeoJSON or flat-array shapes both parse).
@item @code{quakes} --- USGS earthquakes (GeoJSON); no key.
@item @code{launches} --- Launch Library 2; no key.
@item @code{fires} --- NASA FIRMS; needs @code{FIRMS_MAP_KEY}.
@item @code{natural-events} --- NASA EONET v3 (one rich keyless feed:
volcanoes, severe storms, wildfires, icebergs, floods, landslides).
@item @code{nws-alerts} --- US NWS active alerts as filled severity-coloured
zone polygons + a centroid marker (needs a descriptive
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-nws-user-agent}; no key).
@item @code{aurora} --- NOAA SWPC OVATION oval as a band of
probability-coloured dots; no key.
@item @code{gdelt} --- GDELT GEO 2.0 geolocated news for
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-gdelt-query}, sized by mention volume; clustered; no key.
@end itemize

Everything is off by default.  API keys resolve via
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-secret}: environment variable, then
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cmacs/gnuseye/keys.el}, then a default.  A layer
declaring @code{:needs-key} stays off, showing ``needs NAME'', until set.

Wave-2 and keyed layers add ~20 layers across 10 categories: submarine
cables and ports (infra), Safecast radiation and OpenAQ (health), the ISS and
webcams (media), ACLED and a configurable siren feed (conflict), military
bases & spaceports, and a derived @code{hotspots} correlation layer.

@section Interaction and intelligence

Loaded from @code{cmacs-gnuseye-feature-files}:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Search + palette.}  The list search (@code{s}) matches across
all @code{:data} values (callsign / MMSI / NORAD id / country);
@code{cmacs-gnuseye-jump} (@code{/}) is a command palette over entities,
places, and commands.
@item @strong{Stats pane} (@code{S}): per-layer counts, freshness, errors,
on-screen total, kind tally.
@item @strong{Bookmarks & tours} (@code{m} / @code{'} / @code{O}), persisted
under the config dir.
@item @strong{Geofences} (@code{G}): a throttled tick test emits podomation
@code{on_geofence_enter} / @code{on_geofence_exit} events with the entity
data, and can draw a range ring.
@item @strong{Time-scrub / replay} (@code{T}): a bounded snapshot ring with a
header-line scrubber.
@item @strong{AI intelligence} (cmacs-ai, when built): inspector @code{[a]}
asks about the inspected entity (one-shot analysis) and @code{[c]} composes
-- it opens a full @code{cmacs-ai-chat} buffer with the entity's context
pre-filled, so follow-up questions carry the conversation.  Both resolve
the entity @emph{shown in the inspector}, so they work on marker
selections, clicked countries (population/GDP/World-Bank rows feed the
prompt), and celestial bodies alike.  @code{A} briefs the region; the
@code{hotspots} layer correlates co-located signals; @code{C} computes the
country-instability index and paints it as a choropleth.
@item @strong{Follow + click-through}: @code{.} tracks the selection;
inspector @code{[w]} opens a live stream (cmacs-video), @code{[n]} an article
(gsurf).  Inspector actions are bound in Evil normal/motion state too, and
@code{C-w} in the globe viewport is the Evil window map (Doom window
movement works from the render window).
@item @strong{Country details}: click a country (off any marker) and the
inspector shows population, GDP, GDP/capita, unemployment, life expectancy,
inflation, and income group -- instant Natural Earth figures plus live
keyless World Bank indicators (cached).  Ocean clicks deselect.
@item @strong{Right-click menu}: right-click an entity for a native context
menu (Fly to, Inspect, Ask AI, Compose, Track, Copy coordinates -- the
registered inspector actions, predicate-filtered); empty space offers
Deselect / Back to Earth / Measure.
@item @strong{Orbit the body}: selecting a celestial body re-targets the
camera onto it -- drag revolves around @emph{it} and zoom approaches
@emph{it} asymptotically (floor just outside its surface) instead of
Earth's; any fly-to / home / deselect restores the Earth orbit
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-orbit-point}).
@item @strong{Click dispatch}: every click reports its outcome in the echo
area (@samp{selected X}, @samp{cluster of N}, or @samp{click did not
resolve}).  The C input layer dispatches clicks to
@code{cmacs-libregnum--node-clicked} through @code{safe_call}, which mutes
void-function errors -- @file{cmacs-gnuseye.el} therefore requires
@code{cmacs-libregnum} so the dispatcher always exists.  If clicks ever do
nothing, check @file{*Messages*} for ``Error muted by safe_call'' lines.
@item @strong{Dashboards} (@code{D}): table feeds (markets, macro) render as
side-buffer panels via @code{cmacs-gnuseye-define-panel} — never the globe.
@end itemize

New MCP tools: @code{gnuseye_query_entities}, @code{gnuseye_brief},
@code{gnuseye_add_geofence}, @code{gnuseye_cii}.

@section v2 additions

A second wave closing the remaining worldmonitor gaps:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{2D flat-map mode} (@code{2}; @code{3} returns to the 3D
globe): reproject the globe onto an equirectangular flat map (north up)
and back.
@item @strong{Measurement} (@code{M}): click two points for great-circle
distance + bearing (camera-ray globe pick), with the arc drawn.
@item @strong{Heatmap} (@code{H}), per-layer @strong{opacity}, and satellite
@strong{footprint rings}.
@item @strong{Export} GeoJSON / CSV / PNG; @strong{watchlists} that alert via
@code{cmacs-notify}; a @strong{live-news} panel (@code{N}, RSS, clickable).
@item @strong{More layers}: cyber C2 IPs (geolocated), UCDP conflict,
ReliefWeb disasters; @strong{more panels}: crypto treemap, Hacker News, FRED.
@item @strong{History + backtest}: a disk time-series feeds long replay
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-replay-from-history}) and @code{cmacs-gnuseye-backtest}.
@item @strong{Analytics engines} (cmacs-ai): situation deduction,
market-implication, resilience simulation, country SITREPs.
@end itemize

@section v3: the solar system

The @emph{Solar system} category renders the Sun, the Moon, the planets,
deep-space probes (Voyager 1/2, New Horizons, JWST), and asteroids at their
@emph{true geocentric direction} (above their sub-point, sweeping live with
Earth's rotation) at @emph{linearly true distances} -- 290 world units per
AU, one uniform scale, so the orbital geometry is exact (the Sun 290 units
out, Jupiter 5.2x as far from the Sun as Earth, Neptune at 29.9 AU; the Moon
floored at 26 units, deep-space probes capped at the 9000-unit chart edge).
Every body renders as a @emph{textured sphere} with its real surface map
(Solar System Scope, CC BY 4.0; auto-fetched once into the cache and
converted to PNG in-process via gdk-pixbuf), sun-lit with the same
day/night terminator as the Earth (the Sun unlit -- self-luminous).  The
Earth daymap doubles as the globe's default base texture.  Flat-coloured
spheres remain the keyless fallback
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-celestial-textures}).

Sizes follow one sqrt-law for every body, the Sun included (radius =
@code{6.371*sqrt(R/R_earth)}): the Sun renders 3.2x Jupiter, Jupiter 3.3x
Earth, Venus ~ Earth.  The inspector reports each body's true ratio, real
distance (km / AU), and light-time.

Sun/Moon/planets come from local ephemerides (@file{cmacs-gnuseye-ephem.el}:
JPL/Standish approximate elements + the low-precision Almanac Moon; no
network, validated against Horizons fixtures), recomputed every smooth tick.
Probes and asteroids come from the keyless JPL Horizons API
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-probes}, @code{cmacs-gnuseye-asteroids}; 6 h refresh).

Click a body to fly out and frame it; @code{.} tracks it.  @strong{@code{0}
(or Home) is the ``go back to Earth'' button}: it clears the selection and
returns to the standard Earth orbit view.

@section Architecture notes

Only @file{cmacs-gnuseye-globe.c} (the render half) includes
@file{<libregnum.h>}; everything else talks to it through the plain-C API in
@file{cmacs-gnuseye-globe.h}, mirroring libregnum's @code{Color}-typedef
firewall.  Pure geodesy (lat/lon @result{} xyz, great circles, the subsolar
point, destination points) lives in @file{cmacs-gnuseye-geomath.h}.  gnuseye
adds to libregnum a persistent background-model slot (the Earth sphere
survives marker rebuilds), per-tick and static filled-polygon model lists
(drawn after the globe, alpha-blended, two-sided), a per-node label mode, and
a single click router.

The globe is a sphere of radius 6.371 world units (1 unit @equiv{} 1000 km);
@code{lat=0,lon=0} is at @code{(+R,0,0)}, the north pole at @code{(0,+R,0)},
and longitude winds toward @code{-Z} so east is to the right (un-mirrored).
Satellite positions use an exact two-body propagator from the TLE mean
elements (SGP4 perturbations omitted; accurate over the short windows used).

When @option{--with-cmacs-mcp} is enabled, agents get @code{gnuseye_open},
@code{gnuseye_list_layers}, @code{gnuseye_toggle_layer},
@code{gnuseye_fly_to}, and @code{gnuseye_refresh} tools; the MCP @code{eval}
tool reaches every gnuseye DEFUN directly.
